WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
I am Lily Villarra. I am the Artemis Landing Recovery Director and this is how you recover a spacecraft.

00:09.000 --> 00:16.000
We are here in the well deck of the USS Somerset where we did simulations of an actual splashdown

00:16.000 --> 00:21.000
and what it would take to get the crew out of the capsule and also recover the vehicle.

00:21.000 --> 00:24.000
Behind me is the crew module test article.

00:24.000 --> 00:27.000
You can actually put people in it. It is actually a vessel.

00:27.000 --> 00:31.000
It has all the instrumentation structures that Orion would have.

00:31.000 --> 00:36.000
The DoD Department of Defense is a very large part of our operations.

00:36.000 --> 00:41.000
We have two helicopters dedicated for imagery and we have two helicopters dedicated for the capsule.

00:41.000 --> 00:46.000
We have a whole entire Navy diver team and those are the guys out in the open water.

00:46.000 --> 00:50.000
We do bring some NBL experts that also go in the open water with them

00:50.000 --> 00:55.000
and they're really the guys who will be the first people who open the hatch and then eventually get the crew out.

00:58.000 --> 01:04.000
I'm Tim Goddard. I'm the EGS open water lead for the Orion and Artemis missions.

01:04.000 --> 01:09.000
We are at the Neutral Wynse laboratory. It's an offsite campus of the Johnson Space Center.

01:09.000 --> 01:13.000
We are training the Navy divers and helicopter rescue.

01:13.000 --> 01:22.000
Why we bring them to the NBL is for the very specific and technical nature of how you operate in and around a space capsule.

01:22.000 --> 01:29.000
We teach them the specific techniques they are required to do to keep both themselves safe, the flight crew safe, as well as the capsule.

01:29.000 --> 01:35.000
This is where we start and then we'll push out to San Diego and we do what we call JIT just in time training.

01:35.000 --> 01:41.000
By the time they do the actual real mission, they have hours and hours on each phase of that recovery.

01:42.000 --> 01:53.000
Objective for the day is DOD training. So today we're going to be training our divers and our air crews.

01:53.000 --> 01:59.000
Almost every underway recovery we first start our first day is always what we call in and outs.

01:59.000 --> 02:03.000
And that is really to train the ship on how to handle the CMTA.

02:03.000 --> 02:13.000
We fill up this entire wall deck with seven and a half feet of water and then we do in and outs famillization runs to train the actual military crew that will be on a ship.

02:13.000 --> 02:19.000
We then deploy the CMTA out in the open ocean. We do whatever exercise, training exercise that we want to do.

02:19.000 --> 02:25.000
We actually like to simulate what it would be from splashdown all the way to recovering the crew back to the flight deck.

02:26.000 --> 02:34.000
And then we go into contingency simulations. We actually pretended that we had a hurt crew member and then how would we deal with that from a medical perspective.

02:34.000 --> 02:40.000
The next thing we do is an iteration of recovering the capsule. We've gotten really good at recovering the capsule.

02:40.000 --> 02:43.000
We've been doing that for multiple URTs. We did it for Artemis I.

02:43.000 --> 02:51.000
The next thing we did a full mission profile and that is an entire exercise simulating the actual recovery.

02:51.000 --> 03:00.000
We have all four of our helicopters in play. We have the entire crew suited up in play and then we go through the checklist that was splashdown.

03:00.000 --> 03:06.000
My name is HM2 Rubin Hickox. I'm the deep-sea diving medical technician in the U.S. Navy.

03:06.000 --> 03:18.000
Recovery training that we've been doing, a lot of it has been getting transporting the astronauts safely from the capsule after they land to the ship as well as utilizing the ship to recover the actual capsule.

03:18.000 --> 03:25.000
We have a couple inflatable rafts that we use. One is the stability collar, which is just an inflatable ring that goes around it.

03:25.000 --> 03:30.000
And then front porch, which is an inflatable life raft used to attach to the capsule.

03:30.000 --> 03:36.000
And once the front porch is stable and we can walk on it, that's when we'll start one by one just taking the astronauts out of the capsule.

03:36.000 --> 03:43.000
The search and rescue mid-sex will hook up with the astronauts and they'll bring them up to the helicopter, fly them to the summer set.

03:43.000 --> 03:49.000
And as soon as they touch down, they get medical professionals all over, transporting them into the medical bay, making sure that they're good to go.

03:52.000 --> 04:02.000
This is a dream come true. I think everybody that works for NASA says the exam same thing because it's such a big teamwork type of operation and very complex.

04:02.000 --> 04:11.000
And I just thrive and love on those kind of scenarios. It's long days, long hours, a lot of responsibility, but at the end we all really enjoy it.

04:11.000 --> 04:15.000
I can't always see it come back from the moon.

